1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,185
This is an H-block from Puma Punku, in Bolivia.

2
00:00:04,185 --> 00:00:05,169
Sharp edges.

3
00:00:05,169 --> 00:00:06,070
Flat faces.

4
00:00:06,070 --> 00:00:08,775
Inside corners at ninety degrees.

5
00:00:08,775 --> 00:00:12,300
And it is about fourteen hundred years old.

6
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:17,941
Millions of people have seen this stone online, with one claim attached.

7
00:00:17,941 --> 00:00:18,660
Laser cut.

8
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:19,307
Machined.

9
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:21,966
Built by visitors from another world.

10
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:24,651
So today we do something simple.

11
00:00:24,651 --> 00:00:26,870
We walk the site like engineers.

12
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:32,833
Station by station, stone by stone, with a tape measure and the published survey data.

13
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:36,193
Laser-cut stones, or Tiwanaku genius?

14
00:00:36,193 --> 00:00:39,634
At the end, every claim goes on a scorecard.

15
00:00:39,634 --> 00:00:41,433
Let's start the survey.

16
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:46,793
Station one.

17
00:00:46,793 --> 00:00:47,613
The ground.

18
00:00:47,613 --> 00:00:57,000
Puma Punku sits on the Altiplano, about three thousand eight hundred and fifty metres above sea level, south of Lake Titicaca.

19
00:00:57,466 --> 00:00:59,759
The name means Gate of the Puma.

20
00:00:59,759 --> 00:01:08,500
It was a T-shaped platform mound, faced with stone, about one hundred and sixty-seven metres by one hundred and seventeen.

21
00:01:08,966 --> 00:01:12,353
It belonged to Tiwanaku, a great Andean city.

22
00:01:12,353 --> 00:01:20,633
At its peak, between about seven hundred and one thousand AD, ten to twenty thousand people lived in its core.

23
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:22,840
First measurement: age.

24
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:32,599
Archaeologist Alexei Vranich dug down to the deepest, oldest layer of fill inside the mound and dated the organic material in it.

25
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:37,861
The result: between AD five hundred and thirty-six and six hundred.

26
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:40,939
The stone buildings on top came after that.

27
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:45,233
And under the fill, there was only sterile Ice Age sediment.

28
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:52,366
That matters, because one of the most repeated claims says Puma Punku is fifteen thousand years old.

29
00:01:52,366 --> 00:01:54,500
Keep that number in your pocket.

30
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:55,966
We will test it later.

31
00:01:56,433 --> 00:02:02,946
The first European to describe the site was Pedro Cieza de León, in fifteen forty-nine.

32
00:02:02,946 --> 00:02:06,464
The Spanish called what they saw inconceivable.

33
00:02:06,464 --> 00:02:09,833
Some walls and a gateway were still standing.

34
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,864
Then came five centuries of damage.

35
00:02:12,864 --> 00:02:15,063
Treasure hunters dug for gold.

36
00:02:15,063 --> 00:02:20,266
Stones were hauled away for churches, houses, and even railway ballast.

37
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:25,679
So the first rule of this survey: nothing is in its original position.

38
00:02:25,679 --> 00:02:29,566
As Vranich put it, there isn't a single stone in place.

39
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:31,012
Station two.

40
00:02:31,012 --> 00:02:31,910
The giants.

41
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:41,866
On the east side lies the stone platform, the Plataforma Lítica, paved with the biggest slabs on the entire Tiwanaku site.

42
00:02:42,333 --> 00:02:50,933
The largest slab measures about seven point eight metres long, five point two metres wide, and just over one metre thick.

43
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:58,278
Multiply by the density of red sandstone and you get roughly one hundred and thirty-one tonnes.

44
00:02:58,278 --> 00:03:02,333
The second largest comes in at about eighty-five tonnes.

45
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,108
Now compare that with the internet.

46
00:03:05,108 --> 00:03:08,341
Websites have listed four hundred and forty tons.

47
00:03:08,341 --> 00:03:12,233
One television series went all the way up to eight hundred.

48
00:03:12,699 --> 00:03:17,640
Those big numbers came from early guesses that were corrected long ago.

49
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,398
Real ceiling: about one hundred and thirty-one tonnes.

50
00:03:21,398 --> 00:03:24,600
And after the top two, the sizes drop sharply.

51
00:03:25,066 --> 00:03:26,967
Where did the slabs come from?

52
00:03:26,967 --> 00:03:34,000
Geologists compared the stone, mineral by mineral and chemically, with quarries in the hills south of the site.

53
00:03:34,466 --> 00:03:38,519
They found a match about ten to fifteen kilometres away.

54
00:03:38,519 --> 00:03:45,033
Later fieldwork pinned the main quarry down: a place called Kaliri, in the Kausani Valley.

55
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,324
Moving a hundred-tonne slab that far is hard work.

56
00:03:49,324 --> 00:03:54,832
Proposed methods include ropes of llama hide, ramps and inclined planes.

57
00:03:54,832 --> 00:03:57,433
The exact method is still debated.

58
00:03:57,900 --> 00:03:59,852
But hard is not impossible.

59
00:03:59,852 --> 00:04:03,324
Other ancient builders moved far heavier stones.

60
00:04:03,324 --> 00:04:09,400
And here, the quarry is known, the stone is local, and the slabs are real sandstone.

61
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:10,995
Station three.

62
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:12,286
The famous ones.

63
00:04:12,286 --> 00:04:13,335
The H-blocks.

64
00:04:13,335 --> 00:04:20,433
Grey volcanic andesite, carved into stepped H-shapes with crisp edges and deep recesses.

65
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:22,955
Each one has dozens of faces.

66
00:04:22,955 --> 00:04:29,476
Flat planes, sharp steps, and inside corners that really are close to a perfect right angle.

67
00:04:29,476 --> 00:04:32,666
This is the strongest card in the alien deck.

68
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:39,319
The claim on screen: every H-block is identical, so they must have been cut by one machine.

69
00:04:39,319 --> 00:04:41,766
Let's check that against the survey.

70
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:47,358
The architectural historians Jean-Pierre Protzen and Stella Nair measured them.

71
00:04:47,358 --> 00:04:55,533
Their finding: the blocks are so uniform they could be swapped with each other and still give a level surface and even joints.

72
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,691
But, quote, the blocks do not have the same dimensions.

73
00:04:59,691 --> 00:05:00,228
Similar.

74
00:05:00,228 --> 00:05:01,100
Standardised.

75
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:02,040
Not identical.

76
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:06,066
That points to a template and a skilled crew, not a machine.

77
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:09,323
Here is the clue that explains them.

78
00:05:09,323 --> 00:05:19,399
Tiwanaku carvers made scale models in stone, like the block called the Escritorio del Inca, a miniature of full-size architecture.

79
00:05:19,866 --> 00:05:23,577
Many of these model stones exist in several copies.

80
00:05:23,577 --> 00:05:26,706
Protzen and Nair called it mass production.

81
00:05:26,706 --> 00:05:32,600
The H-blocks were prefabricated parts, made to fit into blind miniature gateways.

82
00:05:33,066 --> 00:05:36,484
Second measurement on andesite: its origin.

83
00:05:36,484 --> 00:05:47,533
This harder, volcanic stone came from across Lake Titicaca, from the Copacabana Peninsula and Mount Ccapia, roughly ninety kilometres away.

84
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:57,179
And on the lake shore at a place called Iwawi, archaeologists found roughly shaped andesite blocks left behind on the route.

85
00:05:57,179 --> 00:05:59,399
Locals call them tired stones.

86
00:05:59,866 --> 00:06:02,968
That is a transport trail with evidence on it.

87
00:06:02,968 --> 00:06:08,699
Quarry, half-finished blocks, a lake crossing, and the finished stones at Puma Punku.

88
00:06:09,166 --> 00:06:10,065
Station four.

89
00:06:10,065 --> 00:06:10,825
The joints.

90
00:06:10,825 --> 00:06:15,733
How do these stones hold together without falling apart in earthquakes?

91
00:06:16,199 --> 00:06:18,602
The blocks interlock like a puzzle.

92
00:06:18,602 --> 00:06:26,566
One trick: the top of a lower stone is cut at an angle, and the stone above is cut to match, so it cannot slide off.

93
00:06:27,033 --> 00:06:34,365
Protzen and Nair also found a thin whitish coat, one to one and a half millimetres thick, on some stones.

94
00:06:34,365 --> 00:06:35,622
Possibly a mortar.

95
00:06:35,622 --> 00:06:39,533
So the famous no-mortar claim is not quite right either.

96
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,385
Now look at the tops.

97
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:45,145
Little I-shaped and T-shaped slots run across the joints.

98
00:06:45,145 --> 00:06:49,433
These held metal cramps that locked neighbouring blocks together.

99
00:06:49,899 --> 00:06:55,783
Chemical analysis shows the cramps are a rare bronze of copper, arsenic and nickel.

100
00:06:55,783 --> 00:07:01,666
In one canal at Puma Punku, the metal was poured molten, straight into the sockets.

101
00:07:02,133 --> 00:07:04,443
And that technique is not alien.

102
00:07:04,443 --> 00:07:10,145
Ancient Greek builders used the same cramp shapes on the Erechtheion in Athens.

103
00:07:10,145 --> 00:07:12,166
The Egyptians used them too.

104
00:07:12,633 --> 00:07:22,440
The same copper-arsenic-nickel bronze turns up in Tiwanaku artefacts across the region, from about AD six hundred to nine hundred.

105
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,533
The metal fits the people and the period.

106
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:26,945
Station five.

107
00:07:26,945 --> 00:07:27,964
The tool test.

108
00:07:27,964 --> 00:07:33,566
If no machines were used, can a human with ancient tools actually carve this?

109
00:07:34,033 --> 00:07:36,072
First, an honest admission.

110
00:07:36,072 --> 00:07:43,776
Protzen and Nair wrote that the crisp inside right angles were most likely not made with hammerstones.

111
00:07:43,776 --> 00:07:46,873
Inca stonework has rounded inner corners.

112
00:07:46,873 --> 00:07:48,233
Tiwanaku does not.

113
00:07:48,699 --> 00:07:52,633
And no construction tools have been identified at the site.

114
00:07:52,633 --> 00:07:57,366
So the experts themselves said: we do not know exactly how they did it.

115
00:07:57,366 --> 00:07:59,633
Remember that, because it is true.

116
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:02,615
So Stella Nair ran an experiment.

117
00:08:02,615 --> 00:08:11,608
She tested blades, flakes and thin chisels of flint, agate, jasper, obsidian, quartzite and hematite on hard andesite.

118
00:08:11,608 --> 00:08:13,666
Bronze tools barely worked.

119
00:08:14,133 --> 00:08:23,733
With stone tools, she carved a half-cross design about twenty centimetres across, with the same precision as the carvings at Puma Punku.

120
00:08:24,199 --> 00:08:27,497
It took forty hours, including trial and error.

121
00:08:27,497 --> 00:08:32,899
The researchers estimated an experienced carver would need about twenty-five.

122
00:08:33,366 --> 00:08:39,484
But one thing she could not reproduce: the perfectly flat inside surface of the carving.

123
00:08:39,484 --> 00:08:43,933
That part is still an open question, and we mark it as unsolved.

124
00:08:44,399 --> 00:08:45,485
Now scale it up.

125
00:08:45,485 --> 00:08:54,233
A state of tens of thousands of people, organised labour, years of work, and specialists repeating the same shapes over and over.

126
00:08:54,700 --> 00:09:01,545
Grinding with sand and flat stones, checking with straight edges, repeating a proven template.

127
00:09:01,545 --> 00:09:03,366
Slow, but entirely human.

128
00:09:03,833 --> 00:09:04,761
Station six.

129
00:09:04,761 --> 00:09:08,166
The alien hypothesis, tested claim by claim.

130
00:09:08,633 --> 00:09:15,966
The modern version starts in nineteen sixty-eight, with Erich von Däniken's book Chariots of the Gods.

131
00:09:15,966 --> 00:09:19,633
Television picked it up and made Puma Punku a star.

132
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:30,637
On one episode, Giorgio Tsoukalos said Puma Punku is the only site on Earth that, in his opinion, was built directly by extraterrestrials.

133
00:09:30,637 --> 00:09:33,233
Let's test what the evidence says.

134
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:36,465
Claim one: fifteen thousand years old.

135
00:09:36,465 --> 00:09:44,470
That idea comes from Arthur Posnansky, who calculated star alignments at a different building, the Kalasasaya.

136
00:09:44,470 --> 00:09:47,600
He assumed perfect alignment to start with.

137
00:09:48,066 --> 00:09:54,456
The radiocarbon date from inside the mound says after AD five hundred and thirty-six.

138
00:09:54,456 --> 00:09:57,463
An assumption cannot beat a measurement.

139
00:09:57,463 --> 00:09:58,666
Claim one fails.

140
00:09:59,133 --> 00:10:01,861
Claim two: diamond drills or lasers.

141
00:10:01,861 --> 00:10:04,514
A laser leaves melted, glassy rims.

142
00:10:04,514 --> 00:10:07,318
Machines leave regular cutting marks.

143
00:10:07,318 --> 00:10:12,700
Nobody has published a single measurement showing either at Puma Punku.

144
00:10:13,166 --> 00:10:22,058
On the same show, Tsoukalos visited a machinist's workshop and admitted the surface looks nothing like a diamond-cut surface.

145
00:10:22,058 --> 00:10:24,334
His answer: it must have eroded.

146
00:10:24,334 --> 00:10:25,828
That is not evidence.

147
00:10:25,828 --> 00:10:26,966
It is an excuse.

148
00:10:27,433 --> 00:10:35,711
And when David Childress held a set square against a block on camera to prove precision angles, the square did not line up.

149
00:10:35,711 --> 00:10:38,066
The block was out by a few degrees.

150
00:10:38,533 --> 00:10:42,950
Claim three: the stones are poured concrete, a geopolymer.

151
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:50,033
Joseph Davidovits published papers in twenty nineteen arguing this, using microscope samples.

152
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:59,950
But earlier petrographic and chemical work matched the stones to natural quarries, and abandoned blocks sit on the route from those quarries.

153
00:10:59,950 --> 00:11:01,425
Casting is not needed.

154
00:11:01,425 --> 00:11:02,966
The claim stays fringe.

155
00:11:03,433 --> 00:11:06,445
Claim four: Puma Punku was a lake port.

156
00:11:06,445 --> 00:11:15,715
Lake Titicaca drains out about thirty metres below Tiwanaku, and its highest levels kept the shore many kilometres away.

157
00:11:15,715 --> 00:11:16,333
No port.

158
00:11:16,799 --> 00:11:19,305
And then the strongest test of all.

159
00:11:19,305 --> 00:11:29,899
In twenty eighteen, Vranich modelled one hundred and forty andesite pieces and seventeen sandstone slabs, and 3D printed them at four percent scale.

160
00:11:30,366 --> 00:11:33,943
The whole model cost about twelve hundred dollars.

161
00:11:33,943 --> 00:11:43,100
When a building was put back together, its design was immediately recognisable from older sites nearby, like Chiripa and Pukara.

162
00:11:43,566 --> 00:11:45,879
Puma Punku has local ancestors.

163
00:11:45,879 --> 00:11:49,610
The design grew out of Andean building traditions.

164
00:11:49,610 --> 00:11:54,833
Vranich called that a solid piece of evidence against the alien claim.

165
00:11:55,299 --> 00:11:56,351
Survey complete.

166
00:11:56,351 --> 00:11:57,600
Here is the report.

167
00:11:58,066 --> 00:12:03,476
Built by Tiwanaku, after AD five hundred and thirty-six: evidence.

168
00:12:03,476 --> 00:12:06,919
Stone from known local quarries: evidence.

169
00:12:06,919 --> 00:12:10,772
Standardised, prefabricated H-blocks: evidence.

170
00:12:10,772 --> 00:12:14,133
Identical, machine-perfect blocks: false.

171
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:20,330
Hand tools match the carving: partly, with the flat inner faces still unsolved.

172
00:12:20,330 --> 00:12:25,263
Eight-hundred-ton stones and a fifteen-thousand-year date: debunked.

173
00:12:25,263 --> 00:12:28,600
Lasers and alien builders: no evidence at all.

174
00:12:29,066 --> 00:12:29,968
The verdict.

175
00:12:29,968 --> 00:12:33,803
Puma Punku is not a crash site of alien technology.

176
00:12:33,803 --> 00:12:42,299
It is a masterpiece of engineering by the people of Tiwanaku, with standard parts, metal cramps and scale models.

177
00:12:42,766 --> 00:12:48,688
There is still a real mystery here: exactly which tools made those flat inner faces.

178
00:12:48,688 --> 00:12:52,566
That is a question for archaeology, not for spaceships.

179
00:12:53,033 --> 00:12:58,064
And giving the credit to aliens takes it away from the people who earned it.

180
00:12:58,064 --> 00:13:02,500
The ancestors of the Aymara built this, fourteen hundred years ago.

181
00:13:02,966 --> 00:13:04,188
That is the survey.

182
00:13:04,188 --> 00:13:08,366
More ancient mysteries are waiting, from the stars to the stones.

183
00:13:08,833 --> 00:13:11,507
Subscribe so you do not miss the next case.

184
00:13:11,507 --> 00:13:18,100
And tell us in the comments: if you could take one instrument to Puma Punku, what would you measure first?
